// Conformance row V110 — control for V109: the loop-head moves that are SOUND
// stay legal, and each is sound for a reason the rule can state
//
// SL-262. V109's refusal is about RE-EVALUATION, so it must not reach a head
// that runs once, nor a condition whose binding the body revives before the
// back edge. Without this row the guarantee could be delivered by a rule that
// simply refuses every `move` near a loop.
//
// Four shapes, and the reason each is sound:
//
//   * a `while` condition whose BODY REASSIGNS the binding — the second
//     evaluation reads the value the first iteration installed, so nothing is
//     consumed twice. This is the same revival `_check_loop_body` has always
//     accepted for a move in the body; the condition joining that window is
//     what makes one rule cover both.
//   * a `while` condition whose body cannot reach the BACK EDGE — the head runs
//     exactly once, so the rule has nothing to say. Reachability, asked with
//     `_block_has_early_exit`, which is the predicate design 15 rule 6 already
//     merges branches with; a `continue` belonging to this loop IS a jump to
//     the back edge and so re-arms the check. WHICH continues belong to it is
//     not the syntactic question it looks like: a nested loop captures the ones
//     in its BODY, but a nested `for`'s ITERABLE is evaluated in the enclosing
//     loop's context and targets IT (review r1's P1 — skipping it accepted a
//     program that destroyed one value three times), while a nested `while`'s
//     condition and a `while let`'s scrutinee bind INWARD. Probed, not assumed;
//     `move_in_a_while_condition_back_edge.saw` carries both answers.
//   * a `for` loop's ITERABLE — evaluated once, before the first iteration,
//     which is why it is straight-line code and passes neither `carried_entry`
//     nor `head_moves`.
//   * an `if` CONDITION — not a loop head at all, and never re-entered.
//
// The back-edge carve-out is HEAD-ONLY and deliberately asymmetric: a move in
// the loop's BODY is still refused even when the body always `break`s. That
// conservatism is older than this rule and is the checker's established
// philosophy; widening it would change answers SL-262 did not set out to
// change. `examples/move_in_a_while_condition_back_edge.saw` holds the
// predicate's own matrix, that asymmetry included.
//
// The `deinit` trace is the oracle: each named value must die exactly once, and
// `fresh` dies once per iteration because each iteration built its own.
//
// EXPECT: success
// EXPECT-OUTPUT:
// drop revived-old
// drop fresh
// drop fresh
// drop fresh
// reassigned 3
// drop counted
// for-iterable 2
// drop tested
// if-cond 1

struct Res { name: String }
extension Res: NoCopy {
    func deinit(&var self) { print("drop {}", self.name) }
}

func consume_bool(r: Res) -> Bool { true }
func consume_int(r: Res) -> Int { 2 }

// A move in the condition, revived by the body before the back edge.
func reassigned() -> Int {
    var r = Res(name: "revived-old")
    var n = 0
    while consume_bool(move r) {
        r = Res(name: "fresh")
        n = n + 1
        if n > 2 { break }
    }
    n
}

// A move in a `for` loop's iterable: evaluated ONCE.
func for_iterable() -> Int {
    var r = Res(name: "counted")
    var n = 0
    for i in 0..consume_int(move r) {
        n = n + 1
    }
    n
}

// A move in an `if` condition: no back edge exists.
func if_cond() -> Int {
    var r = Res(name: "tested")
    var n = 0
    if consume_bool(move r) {
        n = n + 1
    }
    n
}

func main() {
    print("reassigned {reassigned()}")
    print("for-iterable {for_iterable()}")
    print("if-cond {if_cond()}")
}
